# gpt-oss:20b-judge — gpt-oss:20b with a context window that fits a judge prompt (2026-10-06, B190).
#
# WHY: Ollama loads gpt-oss:20b with a 4096-token window (`/api/ps` context_length 4096). B190's issue-readiness judge
# (now a research harness, scripts/readiness_judge.py) sends a rubric plus an issue of up to 16,000 characters.
# Measured on this model: prompt ~= 1450 + chars/3.6 tokens, reply ~450-550 — the largest real issue (EMA-195, 11.5K chars) used 5,075 tokens, so 4K would cut it.
#
# WHY 8K AND NOT 16K: the 20b model does not fit the 16 GB card beside the desktop (~4.5 GB of graphics). At 16K only
# 8.2 of 13.1 GiB sat on the GPU and a judgement took ~70 s; at 8K, 9.5 GiB and ~48 s. 8K holds the largest prompt the
# scorer sends (its 16,000-char cap -> ~6.9K tokens), one slot (`n_slots = 1, n_ctx_slot = 8192`). The scorer also
# refuses any reply that filled the window (JUDGE_CONTEXT in scripts/readiness_judge.py — keep the two in step), and
# Ollama logs each request's `truncated = 0|1` (`journalctl -u ollama | grep truncated`).
#
# Why gpt-oss and not qwen2.5:7b for judging: in the B190 defect test (eval/issue-readiness/) gpt-oss caught 32 of 39
# deliberately damaged sections; qwen2.5:7b caught 9 of 38 and rated nearly everything 85-90.
#
# Served as LiteLLM `wl-judge-oss` (k8s/litellm/configmap.yaml): the Langfuse online evaluators and the B190 research
# harness. (The production issue-readiness check is rules-only and uses no model — docs/concepts/issue-readiness.md.)
# Scoped to that alias on purpose: OLLAMA_CONTEXT_LENGTH would raise VRAM for EVERY model on the card.
#
# Build / rebuild (rogueone):
#   ollama create gpt-oss:20b-judge -f /home/edwardmangini/IdeaProjects/weyland/nodes/rogueone/ollama/gpt-oss-20b-judge.Modelfile
FROM gpt-oss:20b
PARAMETER num_ctx 8192
